<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">White movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as White Army, White Guard, Whites (Russian Civil War)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White movement was the loose coalition of anti-Bolshevik forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. Led mainly by former tsarist officers such as Kornilov, Denikin, Kolchak, Yudenich and Wrangel, and supported at times by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> hosts, its armies fought from the Don and Kuban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Baltic. Divided in its aims and hostile to national independence movements, it was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, and its last forces left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-movement.jpg" alt="White movement" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Personal_convoy_of_May-Mayevsky_dances_lezginka(1919).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement took its colour from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, where the opponents of the Revolution who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy used white, and from the white uniforms of the Imperial Russian Army worn by some soldiers. It was led mainly by conservative officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, who were able to organise an armed movement and shared a military culture. It included republican-minded liberals, monarchists and members of the ultra-nationalist Black Hundreds, and it lacked a universally accepted programme. Its main slogan was a united and indivisible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which meant restoring the imperial borders and denying the right of peoples to self-determination, with possible exceptions for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. In November 1918 the movement united around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Alexander Kolchak</a>, who became Supreme Ruler of Russia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> in South Russia was organised from November 1917 under General Mikhail Alekseyev, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavr-kornilov">Lavr Kornilov</a>, and after Kornilov's death in April 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Anton Denikin</a>. It grew from 64,000 to 150,000 soldiers between May and October 1919, and on 3 July 1919 Denikin's forces began an attack towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, which failed.</p><p>In the east Kolchak led an army with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovak-legion">Czechoslovak Legions</a> and with Japanese support; defeated, the Whites fought on in the Far East until October 1922. In the north and northwest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yudenich">Nikolai Yudenich</a>, Evgeni Miller and Anatoly Lieven acted with less co-ordination, and Operation White Sword in autumn 1919 failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. Remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> evacuated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novorossiysk">Novorossiysk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> on 26 and 27 March 1920 and merged with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-wrangel">Pyotr Wrangel</a>.</p><p>The Whites are associated with pogroms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>, and White commanders sifted prisoners and executed those they judged undesirable in a practice they called filtering. Denikin's troops committed 17.2% of the pogroms recorded, though White officers praised soldiers who committed such crimes, and Denikin did little to stop them. Territories under White control had unstable currencies, and the generals often relied on officials of the old regime to administer them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The defeated Whites went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and built military and cultural networks that lasted through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Organisations such as the Russian All-Military Union and the National Alliance of Russian Solidarists, founded in 1930, sought to overthrow the Soviet government by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. During the Second World War some White émigrés, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-krasnov">Pyotr Krasnov</a>, collaborated with Nazi Germany. Historians such as Vladimir Brovkin argue that the Whites fought for a mythical Holy Russia and saw the struggle in religious terms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “White movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Russian Revolution they fought the <b>White movement</b>, above all in the Makhnovshchina in Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Russian Civil War it served as an unofficial anthem of Admiral Kolchak's <b>White Army</b>, while its earliest known performance by communist troops dates from 1918.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;The Red Army Is the Strongest&quot;, widely known by its opening words as &quot;<b>White Army</b>, Black Baron&quot;, is a Soviet march with words by Pavel Gorinshtein and music by Samuel Pokrass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-red-army-is-the-strongest">The Red Army Is the Strongest</a></li><li><blockquote>It allied with the <b>Whites</b> at times while negotiating with the Bolsheviks.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Disappointed by the <b>Whites</b>' refusal of autonomy, Validi negotiated with Moscow, and in February 1919 the Bashkir army went over to the Red side.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashkurdistan">Bashkurdistan</a></li><li><blockquote>The coast passed to the <b>Whites</b> later in 1918, and Soviet rule returned only in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-soviet-republic">Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
